let's start with the direct pollutants  lead used to be added to gasoline to  improve combustion in automotive engines  it reduced pre-ignition of the air fuel  mixture called NOC however lead is a  potent neurotoxin led emissions from  gasoline reduced the IQ of children  growing up in polluted cities this was  no minor effect lead levels were high  enough in the 1970s to reduce the  average IQ of Americans who grew up then  by five points lead pollution has been  greatly reduced to discouragement  regulations that banned its use in  gasoline and restricted use elsewhere  across the economy the average lead  levels in Americans blood has gone down  by more than a factor of 10 this is one  of the many examples where environmental  regulations have greatly improved human  welfare and have had a positive economic  benefit that greatly exceeded the direct  cost of imposing the regulation next  nitrogen oxides our atmosphere is mostly  diatomic nitrogen which is inert NOx and  other forms of reactive nitrogen are  formed by breaking the nitrogen bonds as  can happen in a combustion process where  oxygen reacts with atmospheric nitrogen  at high temperatures and pressures NOx  emissions can be reduced by altering the  combustion process or by cleaning up the  gases afterwards to remove the NOx  modern cars do both they subtly adjust  the combustion inside the piston to  reduce NOx formation and they use a  catalytic converter to change NOx back  into regular diatomic nitrogen  environmental regulations have driven  the widespread adoption of NOx reducing  technology which is succeeded in  reducing total NOx emissions even as  energy use has gone up but the success  is less dramatic than for land perhaps  because there are an enormous number of  different kinds of NOx emission sources  and the cost of controls is relatively  high  the US has reduced overall NOx emissions  by about 40 percent since 1980 sulfur  oxides differ from lead and NOx in that  they come from sulfur contained in the  fossil fuels as they are produced coal  gas and oil all contain significant  amounts of sulfur  when their extract  from the ground where significant can  mean about 1% or more sulfur by weight  the sulfur contained in these fuels is  admitted to the atmosphere as sulfur  oxides Sox when the fuels are burned Sox  converts to sulfuric acid in the  atmosphere with obvious negative  consequences for human health for  buildings and for natural ecosystems  damaged by acidic rain sulfur emissions  can be controlled by removing it from  the fuel before it's sent to the  consumer and or by using clean up  technology often called a scrubber  scrubbers for removing sulfur typically  work by using a base like calcium and  then making a waste product like calcium  sulfate which in some cases is sold as a  wallboard  in a clever piece of industrial ecology  in the United States typical Sox  concentrations have been reduced by  roughly a factor of five since 1980  other rich industrial economies have  also achieved deep reductions even China  now has strong enough emissions controls  that their sulfur emissions have begun  to decline in the last few years even  while energy use has skyrocketed the  difference between sources of NOx mostly  nitrogen from the air and Sox sulphur  from the fuel has important implications  for control if sulfur is removed from  fuels for example at the refinery one  can be sure that the final users of  those fuels drivers of cars or trucks  will not be emitting any sulfur one  cannot say the same about NOx as the  amount of NOx emitted depends on details  of the control technology in the  individual vehicle for example on how  well a car's catalytic converter is  working even a hydrogen combustion  engine would still produce NOx even  though the fuel has no nitrogen or  carbon the fact that control of NOx  depends on the operation of a myriad of  post combustion control devices makes  regulation and enforcement harder the  final direct pollutant carbon monoxide  is a product of incomplete combustion it  is made when fuels are burned without  quite enough oxygen as with NOx its  production can be reduced by altering  the combustion process and it can be  reduced by catalytic converters located  after the combustion source that oxidize  carbon monoxide back to carbon  oxide since 1980 the US has reduced  carbon monoxide concentrations by  roughly a factor of 6
 

